descriptions.description:
This record provides the complete research artifact for the manuscript Directional Hull Summaries for Budgeted SVM Training: Coverage Limits and Reconstruction-Guided Repair, prepared for submission to Knowledge and Information Systems.

The study examines when random-direction extreme-point sampling can support budgeted support vector machine (SVM) training and where geometric coverage ceases to imply fidelity to the regularized SVM objective. It distinguishes pooled/global, classwise, and reduced-hull geometry and evaluates a reconstruction-guided classwise repair, C-RDS+ReconFill. The accompanying theoretical and numerical analyses include directional-coverage checks, sensitivity counterexamples, and additive objective-transfer certificates based on same-class convex reconstruction.

The artifact contains:



Python source code for all reported methods, baselines, ablations, and numerical checks;

redistributable fixed train/test arrays and dataset provenance metadata;

per-run experimental results, aggregated tables, figures, and solver diagnostics;

ten-seed reconstruction experiments on six confirmatory datasets;

a 1,620-fit component ablation and regularization-sensitivity study;

matched runtime measurements and software/environment records;

scripts for reproducing the experiments and verifying file integrity.


The six confirmatory datasets are Letter A/B, Synthetic, Mushroom, Spambase, Phishing, and Madelon. Results for a9a and Cod-rna are retained as exploratory diagnostics because the corresponding full-data reference solvers did not satisfy the stated convergence criterion. Cod-rna observations are not redistributed because the source metadata does not state a standard redistribution license; the artifact instead provides verified source metadata and a script that reconstructs the exact fixed split from OpenML data ID 351.

The source code is released under the MIT License. Author-created documentation, figures, tables, experimental results, and synthetic data are released under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Third-party datasets retain their original source licenses and attribution requirements.

